written('raft-log-repair','short_answer','Distributed systems','Explain how Raft’s AppendEntries consistency check brings a follower’s log in line with the leader’s.',['preceding|previous|prevlogindex','reject|refuse|fail','nextindex|decrement|back up|step back','delete|overwrite|replace|remove','match|consistent|identical'],['The leader includes the index and term of the entry immediately preceding the new entries.','A follower rejects the request if it has no entry at that index with that term.','After a rejection the leader decrements its nextIndex for that follower and retries.','Conflicting follower entries after the matching point are deleted and replaced with the leader’s entries.','When the check succeeds, the follower’s log matches the leader’s log through the new entries.'],'The consistency check finds the latest point where both logs agree; the leader then overwrites everything after it.')
written('pg-serializable-use','short_answer','Databases','Explain how an application should use PostgreSQL’s Serializable isolation safely.',['serialization failure|could not serialize','abort|roll back|rollback','retry|rerun|re-run','not trust|not rely|discard|invalid','read only|read-only'],['Serializable transactions can fail with a serialization failure.','The application should abort the failed transaction.','It should retry the entire transaction from the beginning.','Data read in a transaction that later aborts must not be relied on.','Declaring transactions READ ONLY when possible reduces overhead and failures.'],'Serializable guarantees a serial-equivalent result only for committed transactions, so the application must be ready to retry.','medium')
written('race-homework','homework','Operating systems','Explain why two threads running counter = counter + 1 can produce a wrong total.',['load|read','context switch|interrupt|preempt','same value|both read|old value','overwrite|lost update|lose','race|timing|indeterminate'],['The increment is separate load, add and store instructions.','A context switch can occur between those instructions.','Both threads can load the same old value.','One thread’s store overwrites the other’s, losing an increment.','The result depends on timing, which is a race condition.'],'Without mutual exclusion, an interrupt between the load and store lets both threads compute the same new value.','medium',dueAt='2026-12-31T23:59:59Z')
written('raft-commit-collab','collaborative','Distributed systems','Work together: when may a Raft leader consider a log entry committed?',['appendentries|replicate','majority|quorum','preceding|earlier entries|previous entries|prior entries','current term','apply|state machine'],['The leader replicates the entry to followers with AppendEntries.','An entry from the current term is committed once a majority of servers store it.','Committing an entry also commits all preceding entries in the leader’s log.','The leader does not commit entries from earlier terms by counting replicas alone.','Servers apply committed entries to their state machines in log order.'],'Counting replicas commits only current-term entries; earlier entries become committed with them.')
written('algorithm-argument','short_answer','Algorithms','Describe what a complete solution to an algorithms problem must establish.',['arbitrary|any size|non-constant|general input','correct','induction|inductive|recursion','asymptotic|big o|big-o','model of computation|computation model'],['The algorithm solves the problem for inputs of arbitrary size.','It argues that the algorithm is correct.','The correctness argument uses induction or the recursive structure.','It argues efficiency with asymptotic analysis.','It states the model of computation used to measure cost.'],'A solution communicates an algorithm, why it is correct and why it is efficient under a stated model of computation.','medium')
